<header class="main-header">
  <div class="book-title">Likutay Halachos &middot; Orach Chaim</div>
  <h1>Hilchos Pesach</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">The Matter of Shi&#8217;vee&#8217;yee shel Pesach</div>
  <div class="subtitle"><em>(The Seventh Day of Pesach)</em></div>
  <div class="halacha-num">Halacha 8</div>
</header>

<p class="no-indent">The mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> <span class="term-def">[circumcision]</span> and <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span> <span class="term-def">[uncovering &mdash; the second stage of circumcision, in which the thin membrane beneath the foreskin is peeled back]</span> is an aspect of <span class="heb-term">bittul</span> <span class="term-def">[nullification of]</span> <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span> <span class="term-def">[leaven]</span>, which are the <span class="heb-term">t&#8217;rain ananin dim&#8217;chasyan al aynayin</span> <span class="term-def">[two clouds that cover the eyes]</span> &mdash; the aspect of Esav and Yishmael &mdash; the aspect of <span class="verse">&#8220;Rebuke the beast of the <span class="heb-term">kaneh</span> [reed]&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Tehillim 68:31)</span>, as brought in the Zohar (see there). <span class="heb-term">Kaneh</span> [means:] the <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;&#1461;</span><span class="heb-term">it</span> <span class="term-def">[the Hebrew letter <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;</span> &mdash; which has two legs descending to the ground]</span> of <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;&#1464;&#1502;&#1461;&#1509;</span> &mdash; you break it [i.e., break one of the legs of the <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;</span>] and make from it a <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;&#1461;</span><span class="heb-term">a</span> <span class="term-def">[the letter <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;</span>, which is like a <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;</span> with one leg broken off, leaving a gap]</span>, and then <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;&#1464;&#1502;&#1461;&#1509;</span> becomes <span class="heb-letter">&#1502;&#1463;&#1510;&#1468;&#1464;&#1492;</span>. And this is <span class="verse">&#8220;Rebuke the beast of the <span class="heb-term">kaneh</span>&#8221;</span> &mdash; see there, in the Raaya M&#8217;haimnah, Parashas Pinchas.</p>

<p>And it is brought in the words of Rabainu, in the teaching <span class="verse">&#8220;With trumpets and the sound of the shofar&#8221;</span> <span class="source-ref">(Likutay Moharan I, 5)</span> &mdash; namely, that one must not allow the thought to become <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> [leavened] through cravings and fantasies, and one must rebuke these thoughts and expel them. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&#8220;Rebuke the beast of the <span class="heb-term">kaneh</span>&#8221;</span> as above &mdash; <span class="concept">&#8220;Rebuke&#8221;</span> specifically. And through this, one goes out from the aspect of <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> to <span class="heb-term">matzah</span>. And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">&#8220;matzah&#8221;</span> &mdash; an expression of <span class="heb-term">matzah um&#8217;reevah</span> <span class="term-def">[strife and quarrel]</span> &mdash; for the <span class="heb-term">tzaddikayya avdin matsusa b&#8217;sitrin achranin d&#8217;la yiskarivu l&#8217;mashk&#8217;na dikdusha</span> <span class="term-def">[the righteous ones make war against the Other Sides so that they will not draw close to the <span class="heb-term">mishkan</span> (dwelling-place) of holiness]</span> &mdash; see there. It emerges that <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span>, which are the aspect of <span class="verse">&#8220;the beast of the <span class="heb-term">kaneh</span>&#8221;</span> as above, are the aspect of leavening of thought, G-d forbid, and one must rebuke them and break them.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> and <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span>, which are the <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span> <span class="term-def">[rectification of the covenant, i.e., sexual purity]</span>, and through this one nullifies and removes the <span class="heb-term">t&#8217;rain ananin dim&#8217;chasyan al aynayin</span> [two clouds that cover the eyes], which are the aspect of foreign thoughts during prayer, which are the aspect of <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span> as above &mdash; as Rabainu wrote in the teaching <span class="verse">&#8220;Say to the Kohanim&#8221;</span> <span class="source-ref">(Likutay Moharan I, 2)</span> &mdash; that the rectification of foreign thoughts, which are the clouds that cover the eyes, is through <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>, see there.</p>

<p>It emerges that through <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span> one nullifies <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span> &mdash; namely, the foreign thoughts, the two clouds, etc. And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> and <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span> &mdash; to remove and destroy the two clouds, <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span>, Esav and Yishmael, the <span class="verse">&#8220;beast of the <span class="heb-term">kaneh</span>&#8221;</span> as above. For <span class="concept"><span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span> &mdash; its leavening is severe</span> &mdash; this is the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">k&#8217;lipah</span> <span class="term-def">[husk / shell of impurity]</span> of the <span class="heb-term">orlah</span> <span class="term-def">[foreskin]</span>, which is completely evil. And <span class="concept"><span class="heb-term">chametz</span> &mdash; its leavening is not so severe</span> &mdash; this is the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">k&#8217;lipah</span> of the <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span> [membrane], which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">nogah</span> <span class="term-def">[luminescence / glow &mdash; the intermediate <span class="heb-term">k&#8217;lipah</span> containing a mixture of good and evil]</span> around it <span class="verse-source">(Yechezkel 1:4)</span>, the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">Aitz HaDaas Tov VaRa</span> <span class="term-def">[Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil]</span> <span class="verse-source">(B&#8217;raishis 2:17)</span>. And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">chametz</span>, for <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> also has in it an admixture of good and evil, for <span class="concept">the difference between <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;&#1464;&#1502;&#1461;&#1509;</span> and <span class="heb-letter">&#1502;&#1463;&#1510;&#1468;&#1464;&#1492;</span> is only a <span class="heb-term">mashehu</span></span> <span class="term-def">[the most minuscule amount]</span> <span class="source-ref">(Sh&#8217;lah, Maseches P&#8217;sachim)</span> &mdash; namely, [the difference] between a <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;it</span> and a <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;a</span>, as above. It emerges that it is close to the good &mdash; so much so that it is possible to break it and make from it the aspect of <span class="heb-term">matzah</span>, through breaking the <span class="heb-term">mashehu</span> as above. And this is the aspect of a <span class="heb-term">mas&#8217;k&#8217;la</span> <span class="term-def">[scale / balance]</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">nogah</span>, the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">Aitz HaDaas</span>, for the <span class="heb-term">mas&#8217;k&#8217;la</span> stands on the <span class="heb-term">mashehu</span> &mdash; namely, the tilting that tips the scale to this side or that side depends on a <span class="heb-term">mashehu</span>, for the expression of the <span class="heb-term">mishkal</span> [scale/weight] is &#8220;equal,&#8221; and when one tips it a <span class="heb-term">mashehu</span>, a hair&#8217;s breadth, then the verdict tilts to that side, and likewise to the opposite. It emerges that the <span class="heb-term">mas&#8217;k&#8217;la</span> depends on a <span class="heb-term">mashehu</span>, as above.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of the difference between <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">matzah</span> being only a <span class="heb-term">mashehu</span>, as above. And therefore, <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span>, which are the aspect of <span class="heb-term">orlah</span> and <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span> as above, are the aspect of Esav and Yishmael. For the Yishmaelim practice <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> but not <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span>. It emerges that they are only the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">k&#8217;lipah</span> of the <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span>. However, Esav is the aspect of complete evil and does not practice <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> at all, and they are the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">k&#8217;lipah</span> of the <span class="heb-term">orlah</span>. And through <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> and <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span>, one cuts and removes them, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&#8220;Rebuke the beast of the <span class="heb-term">kaneh</span>&#8221;</span> &mdash; breaking the <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;it</span> of <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;&#1464;&#1502;&#1461;&#1509;</span> and making from it the <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;a</span> of <span class="heb-letter">&#1502;&#1463;&#1510;&#1468;&#1464;&#1492;</span>, which is the <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;a</span> of <span class="heb-term">Daas</span> <span class="term-def">[knowledge / awareness]</span>, which depends on <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>, as is brought &mdash; that it is the aspect of the <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;a</span> of Avraham, which was revealed through the mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&#8220;Rebuke the beast of the <span class="heb-term">kaneh</span>&#8221;</span> as above.</p>

<p>For <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span> is the aspect of <span class="verse">&#8220;You shall honor the face of the elder&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(VaYikra 19:32)</span> &mdash; <span class="concept">&#8220;this refers to one who has acquired (<span class="heb-term">kanah</span>) wisdom&#8221;</span> <span class="source-ref">(Kiddushin 32b)</span>, the aspect of a <span class="heb-term">kaneh</span> [reed] that produces sound &mdash; the aspect of the voice of melody and song that depends on <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>. And this is the <span class="heb-term">kaneh</span> <span class="term-def">[reed]</span> in holiness. And corresponding to it is the aspect of a <span class="verse">&#8220;bruised reed&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Yeshaya 42:3)</span> &mdash; the blemishing of the <span class="heb-term">bris</span>, G-d forbid (as brought in the teaching <span class="verse">&#8220;A drop that dies in its prayer&#8221;</span> <span class="source-ref">(Likutay Moharan I, 27)</span>; see there). And through <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> and <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span> one breaks the <span class="verse">&#8220;beast of the <span class="heb-term">kaneh</span>&#8221;</span> as above, the aspect of the &#8220;bruised reed,&#8221; and through this it is called a <span class="concept">&#8220;bruised reed&#8221;</span> &mdash; <span class="heb-term">ratzutz</span>, an expression of breaking &mdash; for it stands to be bruised and broken by Yisrael, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&#8220;Rebuke the beast of the <span class="heb-term">kaneh</span>&#8221;</span> as above. And then <span class="heb-term">&#8220;acquire (<span class="heb-term">k&#8217;nay</span>) wisdom&#8221;</span> is rectified &mdash; the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">kaneh</span> that produces sound &mdash; through the aspect of <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span> as above. And therefore Yisrael circumcised themselves on the night of their departure from Egypt, for then was the aspect of <span class="heb-term">bittul chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span>, upon which the redemption from Egypt depended &mdash; to go out from <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> to <span class="heb-term">matzah</span>. And therefore they were compelled to circumcise then, for through this one nullifies <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span>, the two clouds, as above.</p>

<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<div class="section-marker">1</div>

<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="concept">Shi&#8217;vee&#8217;yee shel Pesach</span> <span class="term-def">[the Seventh Day of Pesach]</span> &mdash; the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds &mdash; which came about through the aspect of <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>, as it is written: <span class="verse">&#8220;The sea saw and fled&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Tehillim 114:3)</span> &mdash; it saw the coffin of Yosef, who merited <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>, as it is written: <span class="verse">&#8220;And he fled and went outside&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(B&#8217;raishis 39:12)</span>, as is brought. For the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds is also the aspect of nullification and breaking of the foreign thoughts, which are the clouds that cover, etc. &mdash; the aspect of <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span>, as above.</p>

<p>For there is a <span class="concept">&#8220;Sea of Wisdom,&#8221;</span> and in it there are paths and trails to attain wisdom, in the aspect of: <span class="verse">&#8220;Who makes a way in the sea and a path in mighty waters&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Yeshaya 43:16)</span>. And there are <span class="heb-term">m&#8217;tzolos yam</span> <span class="term-def">[depths of the sea]</span>, which are the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">Sitra Achara</span> <span class="term-def">[the Other Side]</span> &mdash; external thoughts, evil fantasies, the clouds that cover the eyes, etc. And the <span class="heb-term">m&#8217;tzolos yam</span> cover the Sea of Wisdom and confuse the path and trail that exist in the Sea of Wisdom. And one must break them and expel them, and then the Sea of Wisdom is revealed and one attains paths and trails in the Sea of Wisdom to achieve perceptions. And this is the aspect of the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&#8220;Who makes a way in the sea,&#8221;</span> etc. For there they merited great perceptions, as our Sages of blessed memory said <span class="source-ref">(M&#8217;chilta, B&#8217;shalach ch. 2)</span>. And this came about through the sea being split &mdash; namely, the <span class="heb-term">m&#8217;tzolos yam</span> that flood and drown were expelled. And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&#8220;the Splitting of <span class="heb-term">Yam Suf</span>&#8221;</span> &mdash; <span class="heb-term">suf</span> in the sense of <span class="verse">&#8220;reed (<span class="heb-term">kaneh</span>) and <span class="heb-term">suf</span> have withered&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Yeshaya 19:6)</span>, the aspect of <span class="verse">&#8220;Rebuke the beast of the <span class="heb-term">kaneh</span>&#8221;</span> as above. And through this, the paths of the Sea of Wisdom were revealed, as above. And therefore it came about specifically through the aspect of <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>, for the rectification of the mind and wisdom &mdash; to expel the depths of the sea &mdash; is through <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>, as above. And whoever merits <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span> can expel the depths of the sea &mdash; foreign thoughts and fantasies &mdash; in the aspect of the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&#8220;The sea saw and fled&#8221;</span> &mdash; it saw the coffin of Yosef, as above.</p>

<p>And therefore the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds occurred on the <span class="concept">Seventh Day of Pesach</span> specifically, for then is the ultimate nullification of <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span> &mdash; for it is the last day of Pesach, and then is the ultimate, complete nullification of <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span>, to the point that from then onward <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> is permitted. And this is the ultimate nullification in its completeness &mdash; that the <span class="heb-term">k&#8217;lipah</span> of <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span> was nullified and sweetened to the point that it was completely permitted, for the evil was already subdued and nullified completely, to the point that it reverted to being permitted. And therefore the Sea of Reeds was then split, for the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds is also the aspect of nullification of <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span> through <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>, through the aspect of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, as above. And this is the aspect of: <span class="verse">&#8220;Who split the Sea of Reeds into parts (<span class="heb-term">lig&#8217;zarim</span>)&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Tehillim 136:13)</span> &mdash; <span class="heb-term">lig&#8217;zarim</span>, an expression of <span class="concept">cutting</span> &mdash; the aspect of cutting the <span class="heb-term">orlah</span>; namely, the depths of the sea were expelled, as above. And this is the aspect of <span class="concept">the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds</span> &mdash; the aspect of <span class="concept">the tearing of the skin of <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span></span>, for the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds is the rectification of the mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, as above. And therefore, Yisrael then merited the <span class="heb-term">Shiras HaYam</span> <span class="term-def">[Song at the Sea]</span>, for the voice of song is rectified through <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>, as above <span class="source-ref">(as explained thoroughly in the teaching &#8220;A drop that dies,&#8221; cited above)</span>.</p>

<!-- SECTION 2 -->
<div class="section-marker">2</div>

<p>And this is [the reason for] the custom that on the day of the <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, the <span class="heb-term">mohel</span> <span class="term-def">[circumciser]</span> chants the <span class="heb-term">Shiras HaYam</span> [Song at the Sea], and likewise the <span class="heb-term">paytan</span> <span class="term-def">[liturgical poet]</span> composed <span class="heb-term">&#8220;Yom L&#8217;Yabashah&#8221;</span> <span class="term-def">[the piyut &#8220;A Day for Dry Land&#8221;]</span> for a day on which there is a <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> &mdash; because the Song at the Sea is revealed through the aspect of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, for through this one merits the aspect of the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds and [the] song, as above.</p>

<p>And therefore the custom is to throw nuts and types of food at the time that the infant is circumcised. And certainly there is nothing that does not have a root. That is, it alludes to the <span class="heb-term">bizas hayam</span> <span class="term-def">[spoils of the sea]</span> that Yisrael seized. And therefore they throw nuts and other things so that the children will grab them, to allude to the spoils of the sea that they merited through the <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, as above. And therefore, before they crossed the waters of the Yarden [Jordan], which is also an aspect of the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds, Yisrael were compelled to circumcise a second time and performed <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span>, as above &mdash; namely, the <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span> that had not been done through Moshe. For the essential splitting of the waters is through the rectification of the mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, as above. And therefore, in the days of Moshe, when they had not fulfilled the mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span>, it was indeed difficult before him &mdash; the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds &mdash; and they needed to employ the merit of Avraham and the merit of Yosef and their like, who had fulfilled the mitzvah in its completeness. For <span class="concept">one who circumcises but does not perform <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span> &mdash; it is as if he did not circumcise</span> <span class="source-ref">(Shabbas ch. 19, Mishnah 6)</span>. For the essential source of their [the <span class="heb-term">k&#8217;lipos</span>&#8217;] sustenance is through <span class="heb-term">k&#8217;lipas nogah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span>, as is brought.</p>

<p>And therefore the Torah warns in several places about <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> more than about <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span>, for the essential thing is to destroy the <span class="heb-term">chametz</span>, which is the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">Aitz HaDaas Tov VaRa</span> [Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil], the aspect of <span class="heb-term">nogah</span>, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span> &mdash; for through this one cuts off their sustenance from the holy. And therefore it is necessary to write both <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span>, <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> and <span class="heb-term">p&#8217;reeah</span>, for each one has an aspect of severity &mdash; this one because it is completely evil, and this one because all their sustenance from the holy is specifically through it. And this is the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">tz&#8217;richusa</span> <span class="term-def">[the Talmudic derivation demonstrating why both terms are necessary]</span> that our Sages of blessed memory stated &mdash; that both <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span> need to be written. For <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span> &mdash; its leavening is severe, meaning it is completely evil, as above. And had the Merciful One written only <span class="heb-term">chametz</span>, one might have thought [it is prohibited only] because it is fit for eating &mdash; for <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;or</span> is not fit at all, and therefore it cannot derive sustenance at all from the holy. But <span class="heb-term">chametz</span>, which is the aspect of the Tree of Knowledge of Good and Evil, is fit for eating, for good and evil are mixed within it, and therefore there is eating and <span class="heb-term">berur</span> <span class="term-def">[clarification / sifting]</span> in it. And precisely because of this, its prohibition is more severe, for precisely because of this it can derive sustenance from the holy, as above. And therefore the essential allusion to the wicked kingdom is in the <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;it</span> of <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;&#1464;&#1502;&#1461;&#1509;</span>, as above &mdash; for the essential thing is to break the <span class="heb-term">chametz</span>, from which they derive their sustenance, as above.</p>

<!-- SECTION 3 -->
<div class="section-marker">3</div>

<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="concept"><span class="heb-term">meelah</span> on the eighth day</span> &mdash; corresponding to the <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;it</span> of <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;&#1464;&#1502;&#1461;&#1509;</span>, which is broken on the eighth day through the mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, as above. For the eighth day is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Alma D&#8217;Asay</span> <span class="term-def">[the World to Come]</span>, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Binah</span> <span class="term-def">[Understanding &mdash; the <span class="heb-term">s&#8217;firah</span> associated with the upper <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;</span> of the Divine Name]</span>, from which judgments are aroused <span class="source-ref">(Zohar, VaYikra 10b)</span>, and from there the grip of the <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> derives. And <span class="concept">judgment is only sweetened at its root</span> <span class="source-ref">(Aitz Chaim, Gate 3, ch. 11)</span>. Therefore, on the eighth day, the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> is sweetened and nullified completely &mdash; namely, the <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;it</span> of <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;&#1464;&#1502;&#1461;&#1509;</span>, as above &mdash; and then the <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;it</span> is broken and the <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;a</span> is made from it. That is, the <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;a</span> of <span class="heb-term">Daas</span>, the <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;a</span> of Avraham, which is revealed through the aspect of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>. For there [in <span class="heb-term">Binah</span>] is the aspect of the <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;a</span> <span class="heb-term">ila&#8217;ah</span> <span class="term-def">[the upper <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;</span> of the Divine Name]</span>, as is known.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="verse">&#8220;And on <b>the</b> eighth day&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(VaYikra 12:3)</span> specifically &mdash; with the <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;a</span> <span class="heb-term">hay&#8217;diah</span> <span class="term-def">[the definite article <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;&#1463;</span> &mdash; i.e., &#8220;<b>the</b> eighth day,&#8221; not just &#8220;an eighth day&#8221;]</span> &mdash; that is, the <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;a</span> of <span class="heb-term">Daas</span> that is revealed on the eighth [day] through the mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, which is the aspect of breaking the <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;it</span> of <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;&#1464;&#1502;&#1461;&#1509;</span>, as above. And this is the aspect of the complete nullification of <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> on the Seventh Day of Pesach, as above. [For] the Seventh Day of Pesach is the eighth day from the beginning of the nullification of <span class="heb-term">chametz</span>. For the beginning of its nullification is on <span class="heb-term">Erev Pesach</span> [the eve of Pesach], as it is written: <span class="verse">&#8220;But on the first day you shall remove leaven&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Sh&#8217;mos 12:15)</span> &mdash; which is <span class="heb-term">Erev Pesach</span>, called &#8220;the first.&#8221; It emerges that the Seventh Day of Pesach is the eighth day from the first day &mdash; namely, from <span class="heb-term">Erev Pesach</span> &mdash; and then is the ultimate nullification of <span class="heb-term">chametz</span> and its breaking; namely, the <span class="heb-letter">&#1495;</span><span class="heb-term">&#8217;it</span> is broken, as above. And therefore the aspect of the mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>, is revealed, as above, which is the aspect of the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds, as above. For the mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> is in the aspect of the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds. For regarding the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds it states: <span class="verse">&#8220;And they turned back and encamped before Pi HaChiros,&#8221;</span> etc. <span class="verse-source">(Sh&#8217;mos 14:2)</span> &mdash; that Yisrael were commanded to turn back toward the direction of Egypt, so that <span class="verse">&#8220;Pharaoh will say of the Children of Yisrael: &#8216;They are confused,&#8217;&#8221;</span> etc. <span class="verse-source">(ibid. 14:3)</span> &mdash; so that he would pursue them.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of the essence of <span class="heb-term">t&#8217;shuvah</span> <span class="term-def">[repentance / return]</span> &mdash; to rectify the blemish of the <span class="heb-term">bris</span>, as Rabainu wrote in the teaching <span class="verse">&#8220;A drop that dies in its prayer &mdash; from where does its spirit emerge?&#8221;</span> <span class="source-ref">(Likutay Moharan I, 27)</span>. And he answered them: <span class="concept">&#8220;Through the same [way] that it entered&#8221;</span> (<span class="heb-term">b&#8217;haynu d&#8217;a&#8217;al</span>) &mdash; that is, they asked: from where does one extract the <span class="heb-term">nitzotzos</span> <span class="term-def">[holy sparks]</span> that fell through the blemishing of the <span class="heb-term">bris</span>? And he answered them: &#8220;Through the same [way] that it entered&#8221; &mdash; namely, through the same path by which the <span class="heb-term">nitzotzos</span> entered and fell &mdash; namely, through the blemish of the <span class="heb-term">bris</span>. And now, when these fantasies overpower a person and he breaks his desire &mdash; through this he extracts the <span class="heb-term">nitzotzos</span> from within them; see there well. And because of this, everyone who merits <span class="heb-term">t&#8217;shuvah</span> &mdash; which is the aspect of the Exodus from Egypt, in that one goes out from the domain of the <span class="heb-term">Sitra Achara</span> into the holy &mdash; must, by Heavenly compulsion, return and go back and pass through all the places where he was at first, in order to be tested and refined in that very same matter. And this is the essence of <span class="heb-term">t&#8217;shuvah</span>, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">t&#8217;shuvas hamishkal</span> <span class="term-def">[repentance that corresponds in weight to the sin]</span>. And through this, one extracts the <span class="heb-term">nitzotzos</span> that fell at first, in the aspect of &#8220;through the same [way] that it entered,&#8221; as above.</p>

<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<div class="section-marker">4</div>

<p>And this is the aspect of: <span class="verse">&#8220;And they turned back and encamped,&#8221;</span> etc. <span class="verse-source">(Sh&#8217;mos 14:2)</span> &mdash; that they were commanded after the Exodus from Egypt to return and go back specifically toward the direction of Egypt. And then, when the <span class="heb-term">Sitra Achara</span> sees that they are returning and drawing close to them, they say, <span class="verse">&#8220;The way has closed upon them and they are confused&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(ibid. 14:3)</span>. And then they intensify greatly with the power of all the <span class="heb-term">nitzotzos</span> that are within them, and then it is a time of great distress for Yisrael &mdash; and from this very [distress] they will be saved. For afterward, Hashem has mercy on His people and saves them, and then, specifically through this, they extract all the holy <span class="heb-term">nitzotzos</span> that were within them, as above. And this is the aspect of the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds &mdash; the depths of the sea were subdued, and Egypt and the <span class="heb-term">Sitra Achara</span> fell into them, and Yisrael then merited great wealth from the spoils of the sea &mdash; namely, they extracted all the holy <span class="heb-term">nitzotzos</span> from within them, as above.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, which is <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>. And the heretics challenged: If Hashem desires <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, why did He not create man already circumcised?! &mdash; as is brought in the Midrash. And it is found in the words of Rabainu, of blessed memory, in the teaching <span class="verse">&#8220;A prayer of Chavakkuk&#8221;</span> <span class="source-ref">(Likutay Moharan I, 19)</span> &mdash; that one must specifically circumcise him here, in this world, in order to elevate the good from the Tree of Knowledge; see there well. For man was compelled to be created first with the <span class="heb-term">orlah</span>, and afterward to cut it and break it, in order to rectify the blemish of the <span class="heb-term">bris</span>. For all the generations come to rectify the sin of Adam HaRishon [the first man] who blemished the <span class="heb-term">bris</span>, as is brought. Therefore, they were compelled to come with an <span class="heb-term">orlah</span>, so that there would be a grip for the <span class="heb-term">orlah</span> and the <span class="heb-term">Sitra Achara</span>, and afterward one cuts it, and specifically through this one extracts the holy <span class="heb-term">nitzotzos</span> from them.</p>

<!-- SECTION 5 -->
<div class="section-marker">5</div>

<p>And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> on the eighth [day] &mdash; that seven days must pass over him, during which the <span class="heb-term">orlah</span> has a grip, for it is the aspect of the all-encompassing evil of the seventy nations &mdash; in order to be refined through this. And afterward, one cuts them completely, as above. And this is the essential <span class="heb-term">tikkun</span>, as above. For the seven days of <span class="heb-term">orlah</span> are the aspect of the seventy nations, which are comprised of seven. And as Rabainu wrote <span class="source-ref">(Likutay Moharan I, 36)</span> &mdash; that before one merits the revelation of Torah, one must pass through the exile of the seventy nations, as above, and be refined within them. And afterward one merits the revelation of Torah, which has seventy facets &mdash; the aspect of the seventy souls of the house of Yaakov. And this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, which is the revelation of <span class="heb-term">Daas</span> through <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>, as above. And this is the aspect of the seventy <span class="heb-term">chasadim</span> <span class="term-def">[acts of lovingkindness / channels of grace]</span> that are revealed through the mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">mal</span> <span class="term-def">[circumcised]</span>; see Likutay Torah.</p>

<p>For <span class="heb-term">chesed</span> is <span class="heb-term">Daas</span>. And this is the aspect of: <span class="verse">&#8220;And it was on the eighth day, Moshe called to Aharon and to his sons and to the elders of Yisrael&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(VaYikra 9:1)</span>. <span class="concept">&#8220;On the eighth day&#8221;</span> &mdash; this is Rosh Chodesh Nisan, when the redemption from Egypt begins, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>. And this is the aspect of &#8220;on the eighth day&#8221; &mdash; the aspect of the mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, as it is written: <span class="verse">&#8220;And on the eighth day he shall be circumcised,&#8221;</span> etc. <span class="verse-source">(VaYikra 12:3)</span>. &#8220;Moshe called&#8221; &mdash; he who is the <span class="heb-term">Daas</span> &mdash; &#8220;to Aharon&#8221; &mdash; he is the aspect of the <span class="heb-term">chasadim</span>, for Aharon is a <span class="heb-term">Kohen</span>, a man of <span class="heb-term">chesed</span> &mdash; the aspect of Avraham, of whom it is said: <span class="verse">&#8220;You are a <span class="heb-term">Kohen</span> forever&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Tehillim 110:4)</span>. And this is &#8220;and to his sons&#8221; &mdash; they are the aspect of <span class="heb-term">limudai Hashem</span> <span class="term-def">[those taught by G-d]</span>, where the essential revelation of the <span class="heb-term">chasadim</span> through the <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> takes place, as is brought there in the <span class="heb-term">kavanos</span> <span class="term-def">[Kabbalistic intentions]</span>.</p>

<p>And this is &#8220;and to the elders of Yisrael&#8221; &mdash; this is the aspect of the seventy elders, the aspect of the seventy souls of the house of Yaakov that shine through the revelation of the seventy <span class="heb-term">chasadim</span>, as above. Also, <span class="heb-term">zakain</span> [elder] is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>, the aspect of <span class="verse">&#8220;You shall honor the face of the <span class="heb-term">zakain</span>&#8221;</span> &mdash; <span class="concept">&#8220;this refers to one who has acquired wisdom,&#8221;</span> etc., as above. And <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span> is through the revelation of the <span class="heb-term">chasadim</span> through the mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, as above. And therefore the <span class="heb-term">Kohen</span>, who is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">chesed</span>, is commanded regarding the guarding of the <span class="heb-term">bris</span> more than others. And the <span class="heb-term">Kohen Gadol</span> [High Priest], who is a great and lofty <span class="heb-term">chesed</span>, is commanded even more regarding the guarding of the <span class="heb-term">bris</span>, and he has additional holy commandments &mdash; for <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span> is the aspect of the revelation of the <span class="heb-term">chasadim</span> through the subjugation of the <span class="heb-term">orlah</span>, which is the blemish of the <span class="heb-term">bris</span>, as above &mdash; and this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, as above. And therefore the <span class="heb-term">Kohen Gadol</span> wears eight garments, corresponding to the aspect of the eight days of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> &mdash; namely, <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span> in its completeness that the <span class="heb-term">Kohen Gadol</span> merits, as above.</p>

<!-- SECTION 6 -->
<div class="section-marker">6</div>

<p>And therefore <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> is a mitzvah that Yisrael accepted with joy and they still perform it with joy. For <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">bittul chametz</span> &mdash; namely, the leavening of thought &mdash; and then one can pray with power, so that one&#8217;s voice becomes thunders. And through this, joy is made, in the aspect of <span class="verse">&#8220;and for the upright of heart &mdash; joy&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Tehillim 97:11)</span>, as all this is explained in the teaching &#8220;With Trumpets&#8221; cited above; see there well.</p>

<p>And this is the aspect of: <span class="verse">&#8220;<span class="acronym-letter">&#1488;</span>&#1493;&#1512; <span class="acronym-letter">&#1494;</span>&#1464;&#1512;&#1467;&#1506; <span class="acronym-letter">&#1500;</span>&#1463;&#1510;&#1468;&#1463;&#1491;&#1468;&#1460;&#1497;<span class="acronym-letter">&#1511;</span>&#8221;</span> &mdash; <span class="verse">&#8220;Light is sown for the righteous&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Tehillim 97:11)</span> &mdash; this is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>; through this: <span class="verse">&#8220;and for the upright of heart &mdash; joy&#8221;</span> &mdash; as above. And therefore the final letters [of <span class="heb-letter">&#1488;&#1493;&#1512; &#1494;&#1464;&#1512;&#1467;&#1506; &#1500;&#1463;&#1510;&#1468;&#1463;&#1491;&#1468;&#1460;&#1497;&#1511;</span>] spell <span class="heb-term">kra</span> [<span class="heb-letter">&#1511;&#1512;&#1506;</span>], as is brought &mdash; the aspect of the Splitting (<span class="heb-term">k&#8217;rias</span>) of the Sea of Reeds, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, as above. And this: <span class="verse">&#8220;and for the uprigh<b>t</b> of hear<b>t</b> &mdash; jo<b>y</b>&#8221;</span> [<span class="heb-letter">&#1493;&#1500;&#1497;&#1513;&#1512;&#1497; &#1500;&#1489; &#1513;&#1502;&#1495;&#1492;</span>] &mdash; the final letters [<span class="heb-letter">&#1497;</span> = 10, <span class="heb-letter">&#1489;</span> = 2, <span class="heb-letter">&#1492;</span> = 5] equal in <span class="heb-term">gimatriya</span> [numerical value] <span class="heb-term">tov</span> [<span class="heb-letter">&#1496;&#1493;&#1489;</span> = 17], as is brought &mdash; the aspect of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>, as it is written: <span class="verse">&#8220;And she saw him, that he was <span class="heb-term">tov</span> (good)&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Sh&#8217;mos 2:2; cf. Midrash Rabbah, Sh&#8217;mos ch. 1)</span> &mdash; that he was born circumcised. And this is what the Ari z&#8221;l wrote &mdash; that through the mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, one nullifies a decree of eighty years; see there. For through the joy mentioned above, one can nullify the decree, for one knows [the distinction] between before the decree and after the decree, and through this one knows how to pray and nullify it <span class="source-ref">(see there well, in the teaching &#8220;With Trumpets&#8221; cited above)</span>.</p>

<p>And therefore, after the Song at the Sea, it states: <span class="verse">&#8220;For I am Hashem your Healer&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Sh&#8217;mos 15:26)</span>. For the Song at the Sea and the Splitting of the Sea of Reeds is the aspect of the rectification of the mitzvah of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, as above, which requires healing &mdash; as our Sages of blessed memory said <span class="source-ref">(M&#8217;gillah 17b)</span>: &#8220;Why was healing established [in the <span class="heb-term">Shmoneh Esray</span>] in the eighth [blessing]? Because of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, which was given on the eighth [day], which requires healing.&#8221; For indeed, <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>, through which one merits <span class="heb-term">shalom</span> [peace] (as Rabainu wrote in the teaching &#8220;A Drop&#8221; cited above), is itself a healing, as it is written there. However, because the way of <span class="heb-term">shalom</span> is to clothe itself in bitterness, as is explained there (see there) &mdash; and this is the aspect of the pain and anguish of the <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, that one must endure bitterness and pain when one wishes to merit <span class="heb-term">shalom</span>, which is <span class="heb-term">tikkun habris</span>, the healing of the soul, the aspect of <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> &mdash; because of this, one needs afterward a healing upon the healing of <span class="heb-term">shalom</span>, in order to heal the pain of the bitterness in which the <span class="heb-term">shalom</span> clothed itself, as above. And this is the aspect of: <span class="verse">&#8220;Heal us, Hashem, and we shall be healed&#8221;</span> &mdash; that we pray for two healings &mdash; namely, to heal the pain of the healing, the aspect of the pain of the <span class="heb-term">meelah</span>, which is itself a healing, as above. <span class="concept">Blessed is Hashem forever, <span class="heb-term">amain</span> and <span class="heb-term">amain</span>.</span></p>

<!-- SECTION 7 -->
<div class="section-marker">7</div>

<p>Why is <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> on the eighth [day]? So that the whole world should not be joyous while his father and mother are sad <span class="source-ref">(Niddah 31b)</span>. For <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> is the aspect of the revelation of the <span class="heb-term">chosam</span> <span class="term-def">[seal]</span> &mdash; the <span class="heb-term">bris kodesh</span> <span class="term-def">[holy covenant]</span> &mdash; as we say [in the blessing]: <span class="verse">&#8220;And his offspring He sealed,&#8221;</span> etc. <span class="source-ref">(Shabbas 137b)</span>. And <span class="heb-term">chosam</span> [seal] (<span class="heb-letter">&#1495;&#1493;&#1514;&#1501;</span> = 454) is the opposite of <span class="heb-term">niddas</span> [menstrual impurity] (<span class="heb-letter">&#1504;&#1491;&#1514;</span> = 454) &mdash; for they have equal numerical value, as is brought <span class="source-ref">(Aitz Chaim, Gate 35, ch. 3)</span>.</p>

<p>And therefore one must wait until the eighth day, when the blood of <span class="heb-term">niddus</span> [menstrual impurity] is nullified; then the <span class="heb-term">chosam</span> [seal] is revealed, as above. And this is &#8220;so that there should not be sadness&#8221; &mdash; for sadness is the aspect of the blood of <span class="heb-term">niddus</span>, as it is written: <span class="verse">&#8220;I will greatly multiply your sorrow&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(B&#8217;raishis 3:16)</span> &mdash; <span class="concept">this refers to the blood of <span class="heb-term">niddus</span></span>. And therefore the <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> is on the eighth [day], when joy is then revealed, as above &mdash; for the <span class="heb-term">meelah</span> leads to meriting joy, as above. And the joy is in the aspect of the eighth day, which is the aspect of <span class="heb-term">Binah</span>, as above. And <span class="concept"><span class="heb-term">Binah</span> is the heart (<span class="heb-term">liba</span>)</span>, and there is the joy, as it is written: <span class="verse">&#8220;You have placed joy in my heart&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Tehillim 4:8)</span>. And there are the thunders through which one merits joy, as above &mdash; as it is written: <span class="verse">&#8220;And the thunder of His might &mdash; who can comprehend (<span class="heb-term">yisbonain</span>)?&#8221;</span> <span class="verse-source">(Iyov 26:14)</span>.</p>

<!-- SEPARATOR AND SUMMATION -->
<div class="separator">&bull; &bull; &bull;</div>

<div class="summation">
  <div class="summation-header">Translator&#8217;s Summary</div>
  <p>This teaching connects <em>meelah</em> (circumcision) and <em>p&#8217;reeah</em> (the second stage of circumcision) to the nullification of <em>chametz</em> (leavened bread) and <em>s&#8217;or</em> (leaven/sourdough) on Pesach. The core structure is:</p>
  <p>The letter <span style="font-family:'Frank Ruhl Libre',serif; font-size:20px;">&#1495;</span>&#8217;it of <span style="font-family:'Frank Ruhl Libre',serif; font-size:20px;">&#1495;&#1464;&#1502;&#1461;&#1509;</span> represents the &#8220;two clouds that cover the eyes&#8221; &mdash; foreign thoughts and evil fantasies. This letter has <strong>two legs</strong> reaching the ground, symbolizing the two <em>k&#8217;lipos</em> (husks): the <em>orlah</em> (foreskin = pure evil, corresponding to Esav and <em>s&#8217;or</em>) and the <em>p&#8217;reeah</em> membrane (a mixture of good and evil, corresponding to Yishmael and <em>chametz</em>).</p>
  <p>When one <strong>breaks the <span style="font-family:'Frank Ruhl Libre',serif; font-size:20px;">&#1495;</span>&#8217;it</strong> &mdash; by cutting one of its legs &mdash; it becomes a <strong><span style="font-family:'Frank Ruhl Libre',serif; font-size:20px;">&#1492;</span>&#8217;a</strong>, the letter of <em>Daas</em> (holy knowledge), the <span style="font-family:'Frank Ruhl Libre',serif; font-size:20px;">&#1492;</span>&#8217;a added to Avraham&#8217;s name when he was circumcised. Thus <span style="font-family:'Frank Ruhl Libre',serif; font-size:20px;">&#1495;&#1464;&#1502;&#1461;&#1509;</span> becomes <span style="font-family:'Frank Ruhl Libre',serif; font-size:20px;">&#1502;&#1463;&#1510;&#1468;&#1464;&#1492;</span>.</p>
  <p>This transformation is the inner meaning of the <strong>Splitting of the Sea</strong> on the Seventh Day of Pesach &mdash; which was itself the <em>eighth</em> day from the start of nullifying <em>chametz</em> (on <em>Erev Pesach</em>), paralleling <em>meelah</em> on the eighth day. The sea split in the merit of Yosef&#8217;s <em>tikkun habris</em> (&#8220;The sea saw and fled&#8221; &mdash; it saw the coffin of Yosef).</p>
  <p>The teaching further explains that <em>t&#8217;shuvah</em> (repentance) requires one to revisit the very places of one&#8217;s fall &mdash; &#8220;through the same way that it entered&#8221; &mdash; in order to extract the holy sparks. This is why Yisrael were commanded to turn <em>back</em> toward Egypt before the sea split, and why man must be born with an <em>orlah</em> and then cut it, rather than being created already circumcised.</p>
  <p>Finally, the joy of <em>meelah</em>, the healing of <em>shalom</em> that comes through <em>tikkun habris</em>, and the relationship between <em>chosam</em> (seal = 454) and <em>niddas</em> (impurity = 454) tie together circumcision on the eighth day, the Seventh Day of Pesach, the thunders of prayer, and the ultimate joy of the heart.</p>
</div>

